# OTPotato

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:req, "~> 0.5.10"}
])


```

## OTPotato API playground

This is an interactive playground for testing the OTPotato HTTP API. It uses Liveview and [req](https://hexdocs.pm/req/readme.html) to create HTTP Client smart cells.

Please click "Reconnect and setup" at the top of the page to install dependencies. You'll also need to be running the OTPotato API server - you can run this with `mix ecto.setup & mix phx.server`

There are 3 API endpoints. The first one creates a garden with one or more beds, from a CSV input.

You can see an [example garden CSV input here](https://github.com/Verna-Earth/interview-coding-challenge-2/blob/main/example-data/example-garden.csv). You'll need to copy and paste this into the "body" param in the cell below. Also note the "Content-Type" header is set to `text/csv`

## Creating a garden

```elixir
req =
  Req.new(
    method: :post,
    url: "http://localhost:4000/api/gardens",
    headers: %{"Content-Type" => "text/csv"},
    body: """
    "soil_type","x","y","w","l"
    chalk,0,0,2.5,1.8
    loam,5,3,3.0,3.0
    """
  )
{req, resp0} = Req.request(req)
```

**Congratulations on creating your first garden** - if you got a `status: 201 created`. If you got an error - maybe the beds are overlapping, or you didn't create any beds?

<!-- livebook:{"reevaluate_automatically":true} -->

```elixir
%{status: 201, body: %{"garden" => garden}} = resp0

%{"beds" => beds} = garden

bed_ids = beds |> Enum.map(& &1["id"]) |> IO.inspect(label: :bed_ids)

```

```elixir
plants = [
  "tomato",
  "onion",
  "garlic",
  "carrot",
  "pea",
  "sweetcorn",
  "potato",
  "spinach",
  "radish"
]

IO.puts("Example planting plan CSV body:\n")

csv_header = "\"bed-id\",\"plant\",\"area\"\n"

csv = 0..:rand.uniform(10)
|> Enum.reduce(csv_header, fn _, acc -> 
  acc <> "#{Enum.random(bed_ids)},#{Enum.random(plants)},#{:rand.uniform(3)}\n"
end)

IO.puts(csv)


```

## Creating a planting plan

Now we'll create a planting plan.

Take the [example planting plan CSV from here](https://github.com/Verna-Earth/interview-coding-challenge-2/blob/main/example-data/example-plan.csv) or tweak the randomly generated example above. Once again set this as the Raw Body, and check the `Content-Type: text/csv` header is set.

```elixir
req =
  Req.new(
    method: :post,
    url: "http://localhost:4000/api/planting-plans",
    headers: %{"Content-Type" => "text/csv"},
    body: csv # Tweak this 
  )
{req, resp1} = Req.request(req)
resp1
```

Hopefully you successfully created a planting plan - if you had enough room in the beds for all those tasty vegetables!

The planting plan response includes an id - which can be used to get the score for the planting plan in the third and final endpoint

## Scoring a planting plan

```elixir
%{status: 201, body: %{"planting_plan" => planting_plan}} = resp1

req =
  Req.new(
    method: :get,
    url: "http://localhost:4000/api/planting-plans/#{planting_plan["id"]}/score",
    headers: %{"Content-Type" => "text/csv"},
body: csv # Tweak this 
  )
{req, resp2} = Req.request(req)

%{status: 200, body: %{"score" => score}} = resp2

score
```

Thank you for making it to the end of my OTPotato Livebook!

Throughout the project I had a few thoughts of things I might to differently, given more time:

* Plants don't have an id in the background knowledge file, but when I created the model I gave them a generated uuid. This caused more issues because the other endpoints only give/return a plant name, and for some of the logic I have to look up the plant by name to get the id.
* The API response could do with some more thought, I just returned a similar payload to the request but with generated IDs. Perhaps the create planting plan endpoint could also return the score immediately?
* The API can only create new gardens/planting plans - it'd be very useful to be able to edit existing gardens/plans I'm sure.
* The CSV API parsing is slightly non-standard, working with JSON would have been easier with Phoenix out-the-box.
* I really wanted to add Open API specs, using [open_api_spex](https://hexdocs.pm/open_api_spex/3.4.0/readme.html) but I didn't have time. Hopefully the interactive nature of the Livebook has been an interesting alternative.
* I'm fairly happy with the test coverage - I added integration tests at the Controller and Context layer - sometimes I think it's safer to not mock out the data layer. But if performance was an issue it's something I'd consider. Also using Ecto test factories would improve the code readability.
* It'd be nice to add a UI to this app - being able to view the area of plants would be easier to see how much space we have left in each bed.
* Using gen AI to generate a recommended planting plan, for the user to tweak, based on some params, e.g. garden dimensions, soil types.
